(Per-run scratch sandbox, plan 323 T2.

 Confinement is enforced OUTSIDE this file, at the process level.
 scripts/fuzz-confined.sh runs the whole vox-fuzz process inside bwrap,
 with only the scratch root bind-mounted read-write and everything else
 read-only, no network. That is deliberate, not an oversight: wrapping
 each GENERATED program individually (rather than the fuzzer process)
 was tried and rejected - bwrap flattens a segfaulting child's true wait
 status (WIFSIGNALED, signal 11) into a plain exit code (WIFEXITED,
 code 139), which destroys the exact distinction runner.vox's
 'supervise' exists to preserve. See scripts/fuzz-confined.sh's header
 for the measurements behind that call. Every generated program is a
 descendant of the confined vox-fuzz process, so it inherits the
 confinement for free, and supervise/'run shell'/'run program' - the
 functions that make the exit/signal/hang distinction - are untouched
 by this file.

 This file only provides the scratch directory lifecycle: a unique
 per-program directory, created before the program runs and swept after
 it ends, however it ends. Wiring this into the live gen loop (actually
 running a generated program against a scratch path for file I/O) is
 T4, not this task - T2 is infrastructure only.

 Dependency note: like every file in this codebase, this one does not
 'see' its own dependencies - the includer does, in order. A caller
 needs 'see "./harness.vox".' (supervise, 'run shell') and
 'see "./runner.vox".' ('make directories', 'shell quote') before
 'see "./sandbox.vox".' - tests/210_scratch_sandbox.vox shows the order.)

(Uniqueness: every concurrently-running vox-fuzz process has a distinct
 OS pid, and no two programs within one run share a seed, so pid+seed
 is collision-free both across concurrently-running vox-fuzz processes
 and across the many scratch directories inside one run. Vox has no
 getpid() of its own, so this reads it the same way harness.vox's own
 'kill grandchild of' already reads a related pid: as a decimal number
 sitting inside a /proc text file - here, the first field of
 /proc/self/stat.)
To 'self pid'.
    open a file for reading called 'self stat' at "/proc/self/stat".
    On error Return a number, 0.
    a buffer called 'self stat data'.
    Read from 'self stat' into 'self stat data'.
    Close 'self stat'.
    a number called n is 'self stat data's length.
    a buffer called 'pid digits'.
    a buffer called 'one digit' is " ".
    a number called pos is 1.
    a boolean called 'still in pid' is true.
    While pos is less than or equal to n and 'still in pid',
        a number called b is byte pos of 'self stat data',
        If b is equal to 32 then,
            Set 'still in pid' to false.
        If 'still in pid' then,
            Set byte 1 of 'one digit' to b,
            append 'one digit' to 'pid digits'.
        Set pos to pos add 1.
    a text called 'pid text' is 'pid digits' as text.
    a number called pid is 'pid text' as a number.
    Return a number, pid.

(The root every per-program scratch directory lives under - relative to
 the harness's own working directory, matching every other scratch path
 in this codebase (./vf_gen.vox, ./vf_gen_bin, findings_dir's default
 ./findings): test.sh already sweeps ./vf_* after every test, .gitignore
 already ignores it, and scripts/fuzz-confined.sh binds this one root
 read-write and everything else read-only - a relative path is the only
 kind guaranteed to resolve inside that bind rather than outside it.)
a text called 'the scratch root' is "./vf_scratch".

To 'scratch dir path' with a number called seed.
    a number called pid is 'self pid'.
    a text called path is "{'the scratch root'}/run_{pid}_{seed}".
    Return a text, path.

(mkdir -p, via runner.vox's own byte-walking 'make directories' - no
 shell, so nothing about the scratch path can be read as shell syntax.)
To 'scratch dir create' with a text called path.
    a number called r is 'make directories' of path.
    Return a number, r.

(Sweep: delete the directory and everything a generated program left
 inside it. Vox has no directory-listing primitive - file I/O in
 LANGUAGE.md is open/read/write/seek/close/delete, nothing that
 enumerates a directory's contents - so an arbitrarily deep tree left by
 a generated program cannot be walked and deleted file-by-file in pure
 Vox. This is the one place sandbox.vox goes through 'run shell' rather
 than a native syscall statement, shell-quoted through the same 'shell
 quote' every other harness-side shell command in this codebase already
 uses - even though this particular path is harness-generated, not user
 input, quoting it costs nothing. 'rm -rf' on a path that was never
 created (create failed, or this is called twice) is a silent no-op,
 so sweep is safe to call unconditionally.)
To 'scratch dir sweep' with a text called path.
    a text called 'the sweep command' is "rm -rf {'shell quote' of path}".
    a number called 'the sweep exit code' is 'run shell' of 'the sweep command' and 5000.
    Return a number, 'the sweep exit code'.

(Run a program with its own fresh scratch directory: create it, run the
 program through the untouched 'supervise' (so its fate - exit, signal,
 or hang - is read from the real wait status, exactly like every other
 run in this fuzzer), then sweep the directory - always, regardless of
 which of the three fates happened.

 The path is handed to the program as its first argument
 (arguments's first), not an environment variable. Vox has no way to
 set an environment variable for a child it is about to fork:
 'Execute' only ever inherits the caller's own environment
 (LANGUAGE.md, "Executing Programs"), and there is no setenv-shaped
 statement anywhere in the language to change that environment first.
 Piping the assignment through 'run shell' (VAR=value program) was
 rejected for the same reason confinement itself was moved out of
 supervise: running a generated program through a shell can no longer
 tell an exit code of 139 from a real signal 11, and that distinction
 is this fuzzer's whole reason to exist. An argv element costs nothing
 and needs no new capability.)
To 'run program in scratch' with a text called 'binary path' and a number called seed and a number called 'the deadline in milliseconds'.
    a text called 'the scratch path' is 'scratch dir path' of seed.
    a number called 'the create result' is 'scratch dir create' of 'the scratch path'.
    a list called 'the argument list' is [].
    append 'the scratch path' to 'the argument list'.
    a text called 'the verdict' is supervise of 'binary path' and 'the argument list' and 'the deadline in milliseconds'.
    a number called 'the sweep result' is 'scratch dir sweep' of 'the scratch path'.
    Return a text, 'the verdict'.
